WEBVTT

00:00:08.883 --> 00:00:10.876
All About South Africa.

00:00:10.936 --> 00:00:13.418
By Philippa Tracy.

00:00:13.478 --> 00:00:18.102
Copyright Burlington Books 2013.

00:00:18.162 --> 00:00:24.318
All Rights Reserved.

00:00:24.377 --> 00:00:33.816
Chapter 1 The Rainbow Nation Howzit?"

00:00:33.876 --> 00:00:37.098
asked the young man, sitting with his friends in the pub.

00:00:37.118 --> 00:00:38.959
'How's what?"

00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:40.279
answered the stranger.

00:00:40.299 --> 00:00:43.981
'I asked how you are,' said the young man.

00:00:44.041 --> 00:00:46.772
That's how South Africans greet each other.

00:00:46.832 --> 00:00:49.563
You're obviously not from here."

00:00:49.623 --> 00:00:51.954
No replied the stranger.

00:00:52.014 --> 00:00:56.204
My name's John and I'm visiting Cape Town from the UK.

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:59.333
Come and join us, offered the young man.

00:00:59.393 --> 00:01:02.354
These are my friends Samuel and Adam.

00:01:02.414 --> 00:01:05.085
By the way, I'm Piet.

00:01:05.146 --> 00:01:10.288
Welcome to South Africa, the Rainbow Nation said Adam.

00:01:10.348 --> 00:01:16.622
The Rainbow Nation inquired John, how did South Africa get that name?

00:01:16.682 --> 00:01:21.168
From all the different cultural groups here, answers Samuel.

00:01:21.227 --> 00:01:22.680
Just look at us.

00:01:22.739 --> 00:01:24.752
Piet is an Afrikaner.

00:01:24.812 --> 00:01:28.234
I'm African and Adam is Coloured.

00:01:28.294 --> 00:01:33.310
You know John, it's quite amazing, remarked Piet.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:37.172
25 years ago, we couldn't have a beer together in public.

00:01:37.232 --> 00:01:39.631
Why not, asked John?

00:01:39.691 --> 00:01:46.263
Because in those days, Europeans weren't allowed to mix with non-Europeans, explained Piet.

00:01:46.323 --> 00:01:48.484
It's a long story.

00:01:48.544 --> 00:01:52.345
I'd like to hear it if you've got the time, said John.

00:01:52.405 --> 00:01:58.007
Okay, so sit down and I'll try to explain, said Samuel.

00:01:58.067 --> 00:02:04.486
Well, you see, the first people to live in South Africa were the Khoikhoi and San people.

00:02:04.546 --> 00:02:13.626
They lived a quiet, peaceful life as hunters and farmers until the Dutch arrived in 1652 and everything changed.

00:02:13.686 --> 00:02:20.076
There was a lot of fighting between the Dutch farmers, the Boers and the Indigenous people.

00:02:20.135 --> 00:02:23.089
Why did the Dutch people come to South Africa?"

00:02:23.149 --> 00:02:24.350
asked John.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:30.455
They came with a Dutch explorer named Jan van Riebeeck, explained Piet.

00:02:30.515 --> 00:02:42.784
At that time, traders were travelling from Europe to the far east to buy spices, but it was a long journey and many sailors died of scurvy because they didn't have fresh food.

00:02:42.844 --> 00:02:58.694
The Cape of Good Hope was the halfway point, so the Dutch East India Company, DEIC, which controlled trade to the east, sent Jan van Riebeeck and his helpers to build a fort there as a kind of refreshment stop.

00:02:58.754 --> 00:03:04.508
Passing ships could stop and get fresh provisions and medical care on their journey.

00:03:04.568 --> 00:03:08.662
So I understand the first settlers were Europeans.

00:03:08.722 --> 00:03:11.334
But then how did the Coloured people get here?

00:03:11.394 --> 00:03:12.721
asked John.

00:03:12.781 --> 00:03:15.609
I can explain that, said Adam.

00:03:15.669 --> 00:03:25.158
You see, Jan van Riebeeck didn't have enough men to build the fort, so the DEIC imported slaves from Asia and Africa to help build it.

00:03:25.218 --> 00:03:29.552
When the fort was finished, some of these men stayed at the Cape.

00:03:29.612 --> 00:03:34.516
The Coloured people today are the descendants of these settlers and their slaves.

00:03:34.576 --> 00:03:37.338
I see, but what about the British?

00:03:37.398 --> 00:03:38.299
asked John.

00:03:38.359 --> 00:03:40.670
When did they arrive in Africa?

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:46.006
I think the British landed here in 1795, said Piet.

00:03:46.066 --> 00:03:49.290
Did they have problems with the Boer settlers?

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:50.582
Asked John?

00:03:50.642 --> 00:03:52.826
Yes, answered Piet.

00:03:52.886 --> 00:03:56.580
The British and the Boers never agreed on anything.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:05.436
The British wanted to abolish slavery and the Boers weren't happy about that, so they took their slaves and left the Cape Colony.

00:04:05.496 --> 00:04:14.395
They moved to other parts of the country and established their own two republics there, the Orange Free State and the Transvaal.

00:04:14.455 --> 00:04:18.991
But today South Africa is one country, so how did that happen?"

00:04:19.051 --> 00:04:20.241
asked John.

00:04:20.301 --> 00:04:23.353
It took some time, Piet explained.

00:04:23.413 --> 00:04:28.506
Gold was discovered in the Transvaal, and the British wanted that gold.

00:04:28.566 --> 00:04:31.838
So they went to war in 1899.

00:04:31.898 --> 00:04:35.140
That was called the Anglo-Boer War.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.190
So who won the war?

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:38.351
asked John.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:44.309
Well, the Boers fought hard, but the British won in the end answered Piet.

00:04:44.368 --> 00:04:51.178
The British general, Lord Kitchener, put the Boer fighters and children into concentration camps.

00:04:51.238 --> 00:05:05.386
More than 7,000 Boer fighters lost their lives in battle, but almost 28,000 women and children died in the concentration camps as a result of poor food and lack of hygiene.

00:05:05.446 --> 00:05:08.866
That's terrible, remarked John.

00:05:08.926 --> 00:05:11.144
Yes, said Piet.

00:05:11.204 --> 00:05:23.494
So the British took control of the two Boer republics and united them with their own colonies, the Cape and Natal, to form one country, South Africa in 1910.

00:05:23.554 --> 00:05:26.549
And was that the end of the Boers?"

00:05:26.608 --> 00:05:27.749
asked John.

00:05:27.809 --> 00:05:30.991
No, not at all, continued Samuel.

00:05:31.051 --> 00:05:40.126
Because in 1948, the Boers, who were now called Afrikaners, came to power and began a system of apartheid.

00:05:40.186 --> 00:05:42.835
What was apartheid, exactly?

00:05:42.895 --> 00:05:44.155
asked John.

00:05:44.215 --> 00:05:50.247
It was the system of forced segregation between the races in South Africa, explained Piet.

00:05:50.307 --> 00:05:59.270
It lasted until 1994, when Nelson Mandela and the ANC, the African National Congress, came to power.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:02.691
apartheid sounds terrible, said John.

00:06:02.751 --> 00:06:05.080
How did it affect your lives?

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:11.452
Well, as I said, we couldn't sit here together like this under apartheid, said Piet.

00:06:11.512 --> 00:06:16.776
You can hear more about that from the great man himself, suggested Samuel.

00:06:16.836 --> 00:06:20.669
I've got an excellent DVD about Nelson Mandela.

00:06:20.729 --> 00:06:23.110
You can borrow it if you like.

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:25.352
Great said John.

00:06:25.412 --> 00:06:27.904
Cheers, said Piet and Samuel.

00:06:27.964 --> 00:06:31.728
Enjoy your stay in sunny South Africa, said Adam.

00:06:31.787 --> 00:06:35.083
Chapter Two, the Mandela Years.

00:06:35.142 --> 00:06:40.968
So let's watch this DVD on Mandela, thought John, later that evening.

00:06:41.028 --> 00:06:45.161
He switched the TV on and a deep voice declared.

00:06:45.221 --> 00:06:47.792
Allow me to introduce myself.

00:06:47.852 --> 00:06:52.246
My name is Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela.

00:06:52.306 --> 00:07:02.062
I became president of South Africa in 1994, but my battle against racial discrimination started long before then.

00:07:02.121 --> 00:07:04.650
This is my story.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:10.598
For hundreds of years, the Black South Africans didn't have any rights.

00:07:10.657 --> 00:07:19.120
So in 1912, all the African people united to form the African National Congress, ANC.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:25.912
At the time, South Africa was changing because of the discovery of gold and diamonds.

00:07:25.972 --> 00:07:28.762
The Europeans owned the mines.

00:07:28.822 --> 00:07:35.110
The Africans weren't allowed to own land, so they worked in the Europeans' mines and farms.

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:39.348
They did the work and the white men got rich.

00:07:39.408 --> 00:07:51.068
But things got much worse when the national party came into power in 1948 and introduced apartheid, a system of racial segregation.

00:07:51.128 --> 00:07:53.590
Apartheid laws.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:08:01.556
All South Africans must register according to their race, white, Indian, colored or black.

00:08:01.616 --> 00:08:04.298
Only whites can vote.

00:08:04.358 --> 00:08:10.504
Europeans aren't permitted to marry a person of any other racial group.

00:08:10.564 --> 00:08:18.092
No sexual act may be committed between a European and a person of any other racial group.

00:08:18.152 --> 00:08:22.336
Each racial group must live in its own area.

00:08:22.395 --> 00:08:25.821
Blacks must live in the tribal homelands.

00:08:25.881 --> 00:08:32.026
All blacks must carry passbooks, identity cards, at all times.

00:08:32.086 --> 00:08:36.178
If not, they may be arrested and imprisoned.

00:08:36.238 --> 00:08:39.500
These laws were very humiliating.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:46.924
At the post office, in shops or at a bus stop, I couldn't stand in the same line as a European.

00:08:46.983 --> 00:08:53.796
In the parks, there were separate benches and toilets for Europeans and non-Europeans.

00:08:53.856 --> 00:08:58.428
We were treated like dirt, and I was furious inside.

00:08:58.488 --> 00:09:08.034
I wanted to take action, so I founded the ANC Youth League together with my colleagues, Walter Sisulu and Oliver Tambo.

00:09:08.094 --> 00:09:10.916
We fought for the rights of our people.

00:09:10.976 --> 00:09:14.137
Don't think the government didn't try to stop us.

00:09:14.198 --> 00:09:20.342
Many of our leaders were living under house arrest or were imprisoned, and I was one of them.

00:09:20.402 --> 00:09:24.104
Later, the ANC was made illegal.

00:09:24.164 --> 00:09:29.476
I remember meeting Walter and Oliver in secret to plan our attacks.

00:09:29.536 --> 00:09:39.840
Then, in 1963, I was arrested for sabotage and imprisoned on Robben Island off the coast of Cape Town.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:45.243
For 18 years, I slept in a 5 meter square cell.

00:09:45.304 --> 00:09:48.858
My bed was a thin straw mat on the floor.

00:09:48.918 --> 00:09:54.883
The food rations were small, and the work in the limestone quarry was hard.

00:09:54.943 --> 00:10:00.748
I was only allowed one visitor and one letter every six months.

00:10:00.808 --> 00:10:08.316
Even worse, when my eldest son and my mother died, I wasn't permitted to attend their funerals.

00:10:08.377 --> 00:10:10.810
It broke my heart.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:20.526
In 1976, while I was in prison, something terrible happened in Soweto, a black township near Johannesburg.

00:10:20.586 --> 00:10:25.928
The black children were demonstrating because they didn't want to be taught in Afrikaans.

00:10:25.988 --> 00:10:30.280
It was a peaceful demonstration until the police arrived.

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:38.358
The children started to sing their national anthem in their language, and then the police started to shoot.

00:10:38.418 --> 00:10:42.654
They killed a 12-year-old boy called Hector Pieterson.

00:10:42.714 --> 00:10:48.096
The next day, his picture was in every newspaper around the world.

00:10:48.156 --> 00:10:52.148
I felt terribly sorry for poor, innocent Hector.

00:10:52.208 --> 00:10:56.900
He was only one of hundreds of victims of violence.

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:58.751
When P.W.

00:10:58.811 --> 00:11:07.118
Butter became Prime Minister, he promised to change things, but he was determined to prevent black supremacy.

00:11:07.178 --> 00:11:15.226
During the eleven years he was Prime Minister, nothing changed for my people, and I was happy when he resigned.

00:11:15.286 --> 00:11:16.416
F.W.

00:11:16.456 --> 00:11:24.680
de Klerk replaced P. W. Botha and fooled me with hope, because his new policy was one man, one vote.

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:31.883
He immediately made changes in the country, setting political prisoners, including ANC leaders, free.

00:11:31.943 --> 00:11:34.613
Everyone except me.

00:11:34.672 --> 00:11:38.052
Then, one day, he sent for me.

00:11:38.112 --> 00:11:42.044
I was taken secretly into his office in Cape Town.

00:11:42.104 --> 00:11:46.836
We were alone, and we sat for a moment just looking at each other.

00:11:46.897 --> 00:11:54.640
It was our first meeting face to face, and I could tell he was impressed by my dignity and self-confidence.

00:11:54.700 --> 00:12:00.094
By the end of the meeting, we both knew we could do business together.

00:12:00.154 --> 00:12:06.268
Next time we meet, we'll discuss ending your imprisonment," he promised me.

00:12:06.328 --> 00:12:11.882
F. W. de Klerk worked on his new policy for one year and kept it a secret.

00:12:11.942 --> 00:12:18.436
Then, on the 2nd of February 1990, the day of revelation arrived.

00:12:18.496 --> 00:12:25.194
I was now living in a private home on the grounds of Victor Verster Prison near Cape Town.

00:12:25.254 --> 00:12:30.824
There I sat glued to the TV screen as F. W. de Klerk began his speech.

00:12:30.884 --> 00:12:33.177
I'll never forget that day.

00:12:33.237 --> 00:12:39.861
In one action, he announced the end of apartheid and changed South Africa immediately.

00:12:39.921 --> 00:12:53.552
The members of my party were free to carry our green, yellow and black flag, and nine days later, I, prisoner, 46664, was going to walk free.

00:12:53.613 --> 00:12:56.154
I couldn't believe it.

00:12:56.214 --> 00:13:03.826
On the 11th of February 1990, I was set free from prison after 27 years.

00:13:03.886 --> 00:13:10.158
The world was a different place, and it took me some time to adapt to my freedom.

00:13:10.218 --> 00:13:19.574
Four years later, the first democratic elections took place, and I became South Africa's first black president.

00:13:19.634 --> 00:13:29.043
My long walk to freedom was over, and my dream of a democratic South Africa was finally coming true.

00:13:29.103 --> 00:13:38.588
Chapter 3 People and Cultures of South Africa South Africa is home to over 48 million people.

00:13:38.647 --> 00:13:47.168
Almost 77% of the people are black or African, 11% are white and 9% are coloured.

00:13:47.228 --> 00:13:51.852
People have mixed African, Asian and European descent.

00:13:51.912 --> 00:13:55.695
Just over half the population live in cities.

00:13:55.755 --> 00:14:04.650
Three-quarters of the population are Christian, and the rest are Hindus, Muslims, Jews, or people without a religion.

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:11.628
The African people are from many different tribes, but most speak Zulu and Xhosa.

00:14:11.688 --> 00:14:18.496
There are 11 officially recognized languages and most are indigenous to South Africa.

00:14:18.556 --> 00:14:24.690
With this mix of cultures and languages, you might wonder how people understand each other.

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:29.222
English is widely spoken and understood by most people.

00:14:29.282 --> 00:14:34.686
English is the language of business, politics, tourism and the media.

00:14:34.746 --> 00:14:41.672
Road signs and official forms are always in English and the President makes his speeches in English.

00:14:41.732 --> 00:14:46.723
Let's meet some of the different people who form the modern South Africa.

00:14:46.783 --> 00:14:48.086
Howzit?

00:14:48.146 --> 00:14:54.380
My name is Emily and English is my mother tongue, like many of the English speakers in South Africa.

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:57.171
I live in a big city called Johannesburg.

00:14:57.232 --> 00:15:04.156
I'm of British descent and I love eating fish and chips, but my culture is very different from the British one.

00:15:04.216 --> 00:15:08.419
I live in a beautiful home and I've got maids who cook and clean.

00:15:08.479 --> 00:15:16.410
Some say we live in a golden cage because we must protect our homes against crime with barbed wire and electric gates.

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:23.601
My name is Willem, and I'm one of about three million white Afrikaners in South Africa today.

00:15:23.621 --> 00:15:26.334
I speak Afrikaans.

00:15:26.394 --> 00:15:29.816
In Dutch, Afrikaner means African.

00:15:29.876 --> 00:15:36.721
Although our ancestors were Dutch, French and German settlers, we've got our own culture and language.

00:15:36.781 --> 00:15:43.676
Most Afrikaans words come from Dutch, but the slaves and colonists also influenced it a lot.

00:15:43.736 --> 00:15:48.839
English words like a apartheid trek and world-beast come from Afrikaans.

00:15:48.898 --> 00:15:53.161
My dad is a farmer from Paarl, a town in the western Cape.

00:15:53.222 --> 00:15:59.086
Paarl because Nelson Mandela spent three years there at the Victor Verster Prison.

00:15:59.146 --> 00:16:03.048
My name is Shaka and I speak Zulu.

00:16:03.108 --> 00:16:07.552
I'm named after the great Zulu warrior, Shaka Zulu.

00:16:07.612 --> 00:16:12.288
I love singing and dancing and I'm a great storyteller.

00:16:12.348 --> 00:16:16.353
The Zulus are the largest African group in South Africa.

00:16:16.413 --> 00:16:21.666
We are part of the Nguni people and our ancestors came from East Africa.

00:16:21.726 --> 00:16:25.329
We believe in ancestral spirits and magic.

00:16:25.388 --> 00:16:31.362
My name is Pat and I'm from the Transkei, a region in the Eastern Cape.

00:16:31.422 --> 00:16:32.864
I'm Xhosa.

00:16:32.924 --> 00:16:39.262
The Khoikhoi and San and San people are part of my tribe and that's where the name Xhosa comes from.

00:16:39.322 --> 00:16:46.330
I speak Xhosa, a language full of clicks because of the influence of Khoikhoi and San words.

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:52.176
I laugh when foreigners try to speak Xhosa because it's difficult for them to pronounce the clicks.

00:16:52.236 --> 00:16:58.456
I believe in an evil spirit called the Tokoloshe, and I visit a witch doctor when I'm ill.

00:16:58.516 --> 00:17:02.678
I believe he can help me more than a traditional doctor.

00:17:02.738 --> 00:17:08.637
I'm Fatima, and I live in the Bo-Kaap, a colourful Cape Town neighbourhood.

00:17:08.697 --> 00:17:13.829
I speak Afrikaans, and English, or a combination of the two.

00:17:13.889 --> 00:17:26.103
I'm part of a very small group called the Cape Malay Colours, where the descendants of slaves from Indonesia, Mozambique and Madagascar, and we follow Islam.

00:17:26.163 --> 00:17:30.595
We love music, and have developed our own style of music.

00:17:30.655 --> 00:17:35.336
you can hear it at the lively Cape Minstrel Carnival in Cape Town.

00:17:35.396 --> 00:17:40.197
There, people wear costumes and sing and dance in the streets.

00:17:40.257 --> 00:17:49.529
Chapter 4 Beliefs, myths and legends Magic and superstition are an important part of South African culture.

00:17:49.589 --> 00:17:54.540
Here are 4 widely held beliefs which still exist today.

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:56.412
Witch doctors?

00:17:56.472 --> 00:18:00.947
The belief in witch doctors is very strong in South Africa.

00:18:01.007 --> 00:18:08.194
In fact, about 60% of South Africans visit a witch doctor when they are ill or have problems.

00:18:08.254 --> 00:18:14.070
There are eight times more traditional healers than medical doctors in South Africa.

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:17.418
Witch doctors can be men or women.

00:18:17.478 --> 00:18:23.536
Some witch doctors perform spiritual healing by getting advice from their ancestors.

00:18:23.596 --> 00:18:27.718
They do this in a ceremony called Throwing the Bones.

00:18:27.778 --> 00:18:37.172
They throw bones, animal bones, shells or stones and interpret their ancestors' messages from the way the bones fall.

00:18:37.232 --> 00:18:43.188
Many witch doctors use medicine called Muti, the Zulu word for tree.

00:18:43.248 --> 00:18:50.557
This is made from natural products to cure illnesses or even to help a person find love.

00:18:50.617 --> 00:18:57.704
In Johannesburg there are multi-markets that sell strange objects for use and traditional healing.

00:18:57.764 --> 00:19:02.418
Mutti can contain ingredients like dried animal parts.

00:19:02.478 --> 00:19:04.559
The Rain Queen.

00:19:04.619 --> 00:19:14.206
The Balobedu people of the Limpopo Province believe that the success of their agriculture depends on a woman called The Rain Queen.

00:19:14.267 --> 00:19:21.392
They believe that this mysterious woman has special powers to control the clouds and rainfall.

00:19:21.452 --> 00:19:29.942
In November, the annual Rain Making ceremony takes place at the Rain Queen's Royal Palace in Khetlhakone village.

00:19:30.002 --> 00:19:38.394
People believe that she can bring the rain for agriculture through rituals and by asking her divine ancestors for help.

00:19:38.454 --> 00:19:45.290
She has to provide rain for 150 villages and her people fear and respect her.

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:53.214
Her own gardens are green and have dense vegetation and rare trees as a symbol of her powers.

00:19:53.274 --> 00:20:01.506
The Rain Queen has many distinguished servants, all of them from villages all over the Balobedu Kingdom.

00:20:01.566 --> 00:20:05.638
They usually come from the houses of the village chiefs.

00:20:05.698 --> 00:20:15.293
The Rain Queen is not permitted to get married, but she can have children with honorable men, and these men will provide good successors.

00:20:15.353 --> 00:20:22.828
According to custom, the Rain Queen can only communicate with her people through her male counselors.

00:20:22.888 --> 00:20:31.351
The last queen, Makobo Constance Modjadji, was the sixth and youngest Rain Queen in the history of the Balobedu.

00:20:31.411 --> 00:20:37.523
But she died on the 12th of June 2005 at the age of 27.

00:20:37.583 --> 00:20:42.945
She left a baby daughter, but the little girl was too young to take the throne.

00:20:43.005 --> 00:20:47.142
This usually passes from mother to the eldest daughter.

00:20:47.202 --> 00:20:54.769
A Rain Queen's death is supposed to bring natural disasters such as drought, famine and disease.

00:20:54.830 --> 00:21:04.255
The Tokoloshe In black folklore, many people are frightened of a mischievous evil spirit called the Tokoloshe.

00:21:04.315 --> 00:21:09.728
This creature takes many forms but always has a stone in its mouth.

00:21:09.788 --> 00:21:12.701
The stone makes it invisible.

00:21:12.761 --> 00:21:23.100
According to legend, the only way to keep the Tokoloshe away at night is to put something like a brick or a paint can under each leg of your bed.

00:21:23.160 --> 00:21:30.326
Because of this, many Africans raise their beds off the ground so the Tokoloshe can't reach them.

00:21:30.386 --> 00:21:36.520
People believe one of the Tokoloshe's favorite hobbies is stealing milk from cows.

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:41.890
If the farmer wants to catch the Tokoloshe, he gets help from a witch doctor.

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:49.300
The witch doctor makes Muti, and after the Tokoloshe touches this potion, it becomes visible.

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:52.552
The witch doctor can then kill it.

00:21:52.612 --> 00:22:10.274
The legend of Jan van Hunks During the summer months when the southeast wind blows, Cape Town's most famous tourist attraction, Table Mountain, is covered with a white cloud which some people call a tablecloth.

00:22:10.334 --> 00:22:22.002
According to an ancient myth, this tablecloth was created by a smoking contest between the devil and a Dutch pirate called Captain Jan van Hunks.

00:22:22.062 --> 00:22:28.556
Jan van Hunks lived at the foot of Table Mountain and was a heavy pipe smoker.

00:22:28.616 --> 00:22:34.766
Every day he climbed the mountain and sat in his favourite place to smoke.

00:22:34.826 --> 00:22:42.581
One day, the old pirate was surprised to see a stranger sitting in his place, dressed in black.

00:22:42.641 --> 00:22:47.860
The stranger wore a large hat, and it almost covered his face.

00:22:47.921 --> 00:22:54.313
Jan van Hunks was astonished when the man greeted him by name and asked for some tobacco.

00:22:54.373 --> 00:22:57.624
Jan van Hunks boasted about his tobacco.

00:22:57.684 --> 00:23:02.506
Only I can smoke such a large quantity of it, he said.

00:23:02.566 --> 00:23:05.316
Nonsense replied the stranger.

00:23:05.376 --> 00:23:10.980
I can easily smoke as much if not more than you old pirate.

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:16.335
Angrily Jan van Hunks challenged him to a smoking contest.

00:23:16.395 --> 00:23:21.397
Soon huge clouds of smoke poured from the top of Table Mountain.

00:23:21.457 --> 00:23:25.158
The contest continued all day long.

00:23:25.218 --> 00:23:32.411
Finally unable to continue the stranger leaned forward and his hat fell from his head.

00:23:32.471 --> 00:23:37.353
It was the devil himself and he was furious.

00:23:37.413 --> 00:23:42.282
I beat you in the contest devil shouted Jan van Hunks.

00:23:42.342 --> 00:23:49.522
Suddenly there was a flash of lightning and Jan van Hunks and the stranger disappeared into the smoke.

00:23:49.582 --> 00:23:57.604
According to legend, the cloud of tobacco smoke they left behind became Cape Town's famous tablecloth.

00:23:57.664 --> 00:24:07.910
Chapter 5 – Places of Interest The Republic of South Africa is situated at the most southern point of the African continent.

00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:12.655
It covers an area of over a million square kilometres.

00:24:12.716 --> 00:24:21.622
It has frontiers with Botswana, Namibia, Mozambique, Swaziland, Lesotho and Zimbabwe.

00:24:21.682 --> 00:24:27.800
The main cities are Johannesburg, Cape Town, Durban and Pretoria.

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:39.462
South Africa is rich in natural resources and is the world's largest producer of gold, diamonds, platinum, coal and other minerals.

00:24:39.522 --> 00:24:50.099
South Africa has nine different provinces and a very varied landscape including deserts, mountains and subtropical forests.

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:55.714
There are so many places of interest that it is impossible to cover each province.

00:24:55.774 --> 00:24:58.026
Let's look at some of them.

00:24:58.086 --> 00:25:08.439
In the Western Cape, you can find white sandy beaches, forests with ancient trees, nature reserves, vineyards and mountains.

00:25:08.499 --> 00:25:13.080
You can watch whales and dolphins or swim with penguins.

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:20.351
You can even go down to the bottom of the ocean in a cage and observe the great white sharks.

00:25:20.411 --> 00:25:31.130
The sharks sometimes try to eat the metal of the cage, but it doesn't taste good, so visitors don't have to worry about becoming a shark's next meal.

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:35.034
The capital of this province is Cape Town.

00:25:35.094 --> 00:25:46.461
Many visitors to Cape Town take a boat trip to Robben Island and stand in Nelson Mandela's prison cell or ride in the cable car to the top of Table Mountain.

00:25:46.521 --> 00:25:53.312
The Northern Cape is called the Jewel of South Africa because people have found diamonds there.

00:25:53.372 --> 00:25:56.825
But these aren't the provinces only jewels.

00:25:56.885 --> 00:26:00.367
It is also home to a number of national parks.

00:26:00.428 --> 00:26:06.242
The Orange River, South Africa's longest river, flows through the Northern Cape.

00:26:06.302 --> 00:26:13.150
At the Augrabies Falls, the thundering water cascades down from a height of 56 metres.

00:26:13.211 --> 00:26:17.226
The Eastern Cape is a surfer’s paradise.

00:26:17.286 --> 00:26:23.189
It is located on the Indian Ocean and has 800 kilometres of beaches.

00:26:23.249 --> 00:26:35.776
If you like wild animals, you can see the Big Five, Lion, Cape Buffalo, African Elephant, Leopard and Black Rhino at one of the province's game reserves.

00:26:35.836 --> 00:26:47.044
You can also visit some of the battle sites of the Anglo-Boer War, and tour Nelson Mandela's birthplace, the village of Mvezo in the Transkei.

00:26:47.104 --> 00:26:54.702
The Green Garden Province of KwaZulu-Natal, borders Mozambique, Swaziland, and Lesotho.

00:26:54.762 --> 00:27:07.760
three cultures, European, Indian and Zulu meet here, surrounded by the magnificent 3,475-metre high Drakensberg Mountains.

00:27:07.820 --> 00:27:19.526
You can observe Zulu tribes in their natural environment and stand on the sites of many battles between Zulu warriors, Boer fighters and British soldiers.

00:27:19.586 --> 00:27:26.952
The largest city in the province is Durban, and this is also the busiest port of South Africa.

00:27:27.012 --> 00:27:35.876
South Africa's smallest province, Gauteng, also known as the place of gold, is the country's economic heart.

00:27:35.936 --> 00:27:39.648
Johannesburg and Pretoria are situated here.

00:27:39.708 --> 00:27:44.060
The gold rush brought people seeking gold to Gauteng.

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:49.766
At first, they lived in shanty towns, and these later became cities.

00:27:49.826 --> 00:27:58.597
The world's largest diamond, the Cullinan diamond, was discovered in the Cullinan diamond mine, in 1905.

00:27:58.658 --> 00:28:05.794
It weighs 3,106 carats and is now part of the British crown jewels.

00:28:05.854 --> 00:28:16.618
Another major discovery, a complete ape-man skull and skeleton, dating back more than three million years, was found in the Sterkfontein caves.

00:28:16.678 --> 00:28:24.360
This discovery proved that prehistoric man lived in this area long before the first European settlers arrived.

00:28:24.420 --> 00:28:41.994
Chapter 6 Natural Wonders and Tourist Attractions Table Mountain National Park Table Mountain National Park is an area of incredible natural beauty and has more species of plants than the entire British Isles.

00:28:42.054 --> 00:28:49.460
It is home to two famous sites, Table Mountain and the Cape of Good Hope, including Cape Point.

00:28:49.520 --> 00:28:53.221
Many different animals live inside the park.

00:28:53.281 --> 00:29:02.173
Table Mountain is a significant tourist attraction with many visitors using the cable car or walking up to the top.

00:29:02.233 --> 00:29:06.955
Around 800,000 people visit Table Mountain annually.

00:29:07.015 --> 00:29:15.097
Famous visitors to the top of Table Mountain include King George VI and Queen Elizabeth II of England.

00:29:15.157 --> 00:29:31.605
The view from the top of Table Mountain has been described as one of the most amazing views in Africa, overlooking Cape Town, Table Bay and Robben Island to the north and the Atlantic Ocean to the western south.

00:29:31.665 --> 00:29:41.140
Situated in the Drakensberg Mountains, the Blyde River Canyon is the third largest canyon in the world.

00:29:41.201 --> 00:29:51.686
It has very unusual rock formations, especially the Bourke’s Luck Potholes, a series of strange and spectacular cylindrical sculptures.

00:29:51.746 --> 00:29:58.206
According to legend, two million pounds worth of golden diamonds are buried in the canyon.

00:29:58.266 --> 00:30:04.847
Apparently, a man named John Holthausen hit the treasure there in 1905.

00:30:04.907 --> 00:30:10.131
He went to prison for two and a half years because he stole a horse and carriage.

00:30:10.191 --> 00:30:16.966
While he was in prison, he admitted that he and two others hid gold and diamonds from the British.

00:30:17.026 --> 00:30:24.594
Nobody knows if this is really true, but treasure hunters are still looking for the missing millions today.

00:30:24.654 --> 00:30:26.847
The Garden Route.

00:30:26.907 --> 00:30:31.828
The Garden Route is one of South Africa's most popular tourist attractions.

00:30:31.888 --> 00:30:37.320
This route extends for 200 kilometres along the Southeastern coast.

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:45.211
The Garden Route offers many activities from searching for wild elephants to travelling on a steam passenger train.

00:30:45.271 --> 00:30:53.705
Two well-known parks, the Wilderness and Tsitsikamma National Parks, are situated on the Garden Route.

00:30:53.765 --> 00:31:00.015
Many visitors also travel to the town of Oudtshoorn to see the Cango Caves.

00:31:00.075 --> 00:31:07.118
There they tour deep inside beautiful caverns and squeeze through extremely narrow tunnels.

00:31:07.178 --> 00:31:09.504
Bloukrans bungee.

00:31:09.564 --> 00:31:18.968
The popular sport of bungee jumping started on Pentecost Island in the South Pacific as a tribal ritual for boys to enter manhood.

00:31:19.029 --> 00:31:24.111
Vines were attached to their ankles and tied to two bamboo towers.

00:31:24.171 --> 00:31:29.664
Jumpers dived from the highest possible point and stopped close to the ground.

00:31:29.724 --> 00:31:33.456
If they succeeded, they were considered a man.

00:31:33.516 --> 00:31:44.800
Today, bungee jumping is popular all over the world and the highest jumps are in Nepal, New Zealand and from Bloukrans Bridge in South Africa.

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:51.923
Most people look down from the 216 metre high Bloukrans Bridge and tremble with fear.

00:31:51.983 --> 00:31:59.006
But those with a passion for adventure, enthusiastically bungee jump from the world's highest bridge.

00:31:59.066 --> 00:32:05.032
Bloukrans bungee jumpers use highly specialized equipment, so the risk is very low.

00:32:05.052 --> 00:32:11.197
They have to wear a special harness over their entire body because of the extreme height.

00:32:11.257 --> 00:32:14.530
So far, there haven't been any accidents.

00:32:14.590 --> 00:32:22.149
But you may want to say a prayer before you fly off the bridge and fall down towards the Bloukrans River below.

00:32:22.209 --> 00:32:25.022
The Palace of the Lost City.

00:32:25.082 --> 00:32:40.136
According to legend, a lost civilization from North Africa migrated to Bophuthatswana in the northwest area of South Africa and built a royal city and magnificent palace there in the Valley of the Sun.

00:32:40.196 --> 00:32:46.735
But one day the city was destroyed by a volcanic eruption and only the palace remained.

00:32:46.795 --> 00:32:55.897
The ruins of the palace were discovered next to Sun City, a luxury casino and resort about a two-hour drive from Johannesburg.

00:32:55.957 --> 00:33:01.690
Sol Kerzner, the owner of the casino, decided to restore the lost city.

00:33:01.750 --> 00:33:09.505
He spent millions to create his dream, a combination of the Jungle Book and Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom.

00:33:09.565 --> 00:33:16.502
The result is the Palace of the Lost City, the world's largest themed resort hotel.

00:33:16.562 --> 00:33:26.498
Its 350 rooms are surrounded by an enormous man-made jungle with water cascades, pools and a massive water park.

00:33:26.558 --> 00:33:35.062
The golf course at the Lost City is a worldwide attraction and golfers come from around the world to take part in competitions there.

00:33:35.122 --> 00:33:39.586
But visitors are too frightened to take a ball from the thirteenth hole.

00:33:39.646 --> 00:33:40.758
Why?

00:33:40.818 --> 00:33:45.729
Because the water around it contains 38 live crocodiles.

00:33:45.789 --> 00:33:47.220
CHAPTER VII.

00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:50.450
WILD LIFE IN KRUGER NATIONAL PARK.

00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:53.121
KRUGER NATIONAL PARK.

00:33:53.181 --> 00:34:03.504
Welcome to Kruger National Park, an enormous game reserve in the Mpumalanga and Limpopo provinces in the north of South Africa.

00:34:03.564 --> 00:34:11.931
With an area of over 19,000 square kilometres, Kruger Park is one of the largest game reserves in Africa.

00:34:11.991 --> 00:34:14.904
It's even larger than many countries.

00:34:14.963 --> 00:34:25.862
The distance from north to south is 360 kilometres, but this journey takes around 10 hours by car because of animals on the road.

00:34:25.922 --> 00:34:31.344
Kruger National Park is named after President Paul Kruger.

00:34:31.404 --> 00:34:39.204
After the discovery of gold in the Transvaal in the 1890s, many people rushed to the area.

00:34:39.264 --> 00:34:43.224
Many of them hunted animals for their skin and horns.

00:34:43.284 --> 00:34:50.246
President Kruger wanted to protect the animals from the poachers, so he helped to establish the park.

00:34:50.306 --> 00:34:55.193
Kruger National Park opened its gates to visitors in 1927.

00:34:55.254 --> 00:35:07.316
Today, more than 1.4 million people from all over the world visit the park every year, making it one of the top tourist destinations in South Africa.

00:35:07.376 --> 00:35:13.260
Visitors can see the greatest variety of wildlife species on the African continent.

00:35:13.320 --> 00:35:20.010
They pay a conservation fee to enter the park and this money helps to protect the wildlife.

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:32.294
Many visitors stay at small hotels inside the park and go on safari, hoping to see the Big Five, Lion, Buffalo, Elephant, Leopard and Black Rhino.

00:35:32.355 --> 00:35:41.000
At Kruger Park, you don't have to imagine a lion standing under a baobab tree with the African sunset in the background.

00:35:41.060 --> 00:35:42.824
It's real.

00:35:42.884 --> 00:35:45.087
The Big Five.

00:35:45.147 --> 00:35:50.930
When it comes to the Big Five in Africa, size isn't the most important factor.

00:35:50.990 --> 00:35:55.352
What counts is how dangerous it is to hunt the animal on foot.

00:35:55.412 --> 00:35:56.832
Lion.

00:35:56.892 --> 00:36:01.555
There are fewer than 21,000 lions in the whole of Africa.

00:36:01.615 --> 00:36:08.980
They are listed as vulnerable by the International Union for Conservation of Nature, IUCN.

00:36:09.040 --> 00:36:16.593
A lion can run up to 60 kilometres per hour and you can hear its roar for 10 kilometres.

00:36:16.653 --> 00:36:20.920
Kalahari lions are a subspecies in the northern Cape.

00:36:20.980 --> 00:36:24.291
They've got magnificent black manes.

00:36:24.351 --> 00:36:32.010
Cape buffalo Surprisingly, these strong animals are the most dangerous of the big five.

00:36:32.070 --> 00:36:36.961
Cape buffalo have an exceptional memory and they never forgive.

00:36:37.021 --> 00:36:42.012
If you hurt one, there's a good chance it will hunt you down and kill you.

00:36:42.072 --> 00:36:46.793
In Africa, buffalo kill more hunters than any other animal.

00:36:46.853 --> 00:36:49.987
Cape buffalo can even kill lions.

00:36:50.047 --> 00:36:56.902
A buffalo is four times as strong as an ox and can easily turn over a car.

00:36:56.962 --> 00:37:07.033
African Elephant African elephants are an endangered species because poachers hunt them for their ivory tusks.

00:37:07.093 --> 00:37:11.123
The animal’s trunk has thousands of muscles.

00:37:11.184 --> 00:37:17.448
An elephant can kill a lion with its trunk, so it's better not to get too close to one.

00:37:17.508 --> 00:37:20.050
Elephants are good ecologists.

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:26.174
They can consume up to 250 kilograms of grass and leaves in one day.

00:37:26.234 --> 00:37:29.786
But a lot of it is recycled into the environment.

00:37:29.846 --> 00:37:36.729
When the elephant eats seeds, they pass through the animal's digestive system and germinate.

00:37:36.789 --> 00:37:40.962
Elephant excrement is also a good fertilizer.

00:37:41.022 --> 00:37:46.848
Leopard There are around 1,000 leopards in South Africa.

00:37:46.908 --> 00:37:52.926
Leopards can run up to 60 kilometres per hour but not for a long time.

00:37:52.986 --> 00:37:58.959
They feed mainly on antelope and take their food up into the trees to keep it safe.

00:37:59.019 --> 00:38:07.234
Like human fingerprints, each leopard's spots are unique and you'll never find two leopards with the same spots.

00:38:07.294 --> 00:38:17.988
Rhinoceros South Africa is home to almost 5,000 black rhinos, about 40% of the world's black rhinos.

00:38:18.049 --> 00:38:23.429
Rhinos can hear and smell very well, but have poor eyesight.

00:38:23.490 --> 00:38:28.170
These heavy animals look very slow, but they're not.

00:38:28.230 --> 00:38:32.061
They can run up to 50 kilometers per hour.

00:38:32.121 --> 00:38:37.554
The black rhino is the most critically endangered species in South Africa.

00:38:37.614 --> 00:38:47.736
In Asia, rhino horn is used in traditional medicine and poachers will risk their lives to get it for customers in Vietnam and China.

00:38:47.796 --> 00:38:52.852
Poachers enter the park illegally and shoot the rhinos with guns.

00:38:52.912 --> 00:38:56.678
They cut off the rhino’s horn and escape.

00:38:56.738 --> 00:39:03.978
The authorities even use helicopters to try and catch the poachers, but are not always successful.

00:39:04.038 --> 00:39:15.161
The World Wide Fund for Nature is working with South African Wildlife authorities in Kruger National Park to save the rhinos from extinction.

00:39:15.221 --> 00:39:20.475
South Africa is the land of gold and diamonds.

00:39:20.535 --> 00:39:29.100
But although these natural resources brought a lot of wealth to the country, they also brought many social and political problems.

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:39.792
Diamonds 15-year-old Erasmus Jacobs didn't know it at the time, but he was going to change the future of South Africa forever.

00:39:39.852 --> 00:39:48.289
It started in 1867 when Erasmus was playing on his farm near Hopetown next to the Orange River.

00:39:48.349 --> 00:39:54.340
He found an interesting stone and used it to play a game called Five Stones.

00:39:54.400 --> 00:40:00.558
He then gave the stone to his neighbour and soon it reached the Civil Commissioner.

00:40:00.618 --> 00:40:04.697
I believe it's a diamond," the commissioner declared.

00:40:04.758 --> 00:40:11.892
The diamond became known as the Eureka, the first of many to be discovered in South Africa.

00:40:11.952 --> 00:40:18.456
A few years later, more diamonds were found in New Rush, today's Kimberley.

00:40:18.516 --> 00:40:24.850
As a result of this, thousands of hopeful people suddenly appeared to dig for diamonds.

00:40:24.910 --> 00:40:30.931
This led to the establishment of the Kimberley mine, known today as the Big Hole.

00:40:30.991 --> 00:40:35.234
The enormous man-made hole is visible from space.

00:40:35.294 --> 00:40:38.899
It is 215 metres deep and 2722 kilograms of diamonds were found there.

00:40:38.959 --> 00:40:44.505
The Kimberley mine was closed in 1914.

00:40:44.566 --> 00:40:49.960
gold.

00:40:50.020 --> 00:40:57.042
In 1886, pieces of gold were discovered on a farm called Langlaagte in the Transvaal.

00:40:57.102 --> 00:41:02.543
This led to a further discovery of a gold reef 50 kilometres long.

00:41:02.603 --> 00:41:10.986
As a result, people came from near and far in search of the gold and the farm became a big mining camp.

00:41:11.046 --> 00:41:17.462
In time, the tents in the mining camp were replaced with houses, offices and shops.

00:41:17.522 --> 00:41:26.243
The noisy streets were filled with oxwagons and horses, but the loudest sound came from the crushing of rocks in the mines.

00:41:26.303 --> 00:41:28.984
You could hear it day and night.

00:41:29.044 --> 00:41:35.768
Soon there were a number of mining towns, and the area became known as the Witwatersrand.

00:41:35.828 --> 00:41:40.242
The mining town of Langlaagte became Johannesburg.

00:41:40.302 --> 00:41:46.896
Soon it became the biggest town in the Transval, and was known as the City of Gold.

00:41:46.956 --> 00:41:51.590
South Africa became the biggest gold-producing country in the world.

00:41:51.650 --> 00:41:55.832
Rich white men made a lot of money from the gold mines.

00:41:55.893 --> 00:41:59.245
But the African mine workers suffered terribly.

00:41:59.305 --> 00:42:03.968
Johannesburg certainly wasn't a city of gold for them.

00:42:04.028 --> 00:42:05.739
Hallelujah!

00:42:05.799 --> 00:42:09.262
Every day I thank the Lord that I'm still alive.

00:42:09.322 --> 00:42:14.650
I don't know how we're able to work in the gold mines under such terrible conditions.

00:42:14.710 --> 00:42:22.442
The boss hits and kicks us, and we aren't allowed to talk, so time passes very slowly.

00:42:22.502 --> 00:42:26.054
The work is hard, and the hours are long.

00:42:26.114 --> 00:42:27.946
But what can I do?

00:42:28.006 --> 00:42:30.528
I need money for my family.

00:42:30.588 --> 00:42:37.484
Yesterday, the mine flooded again, and my dear friend Simelo was killed.

00:42:37.544 --> 00:42:39.486
May he rest in peace.

00:42:39.546 --> 00:42:43.158
I've developed a skin problem from the dirty water.

00:42:43.218 --> 00:42:52.431
The boss gave us all rubber gumboots to protect our feet, but that's only because he doesn't want to spend money on a drainage system.

00:42:52.492 --> 00:43:00.165
The boots don't help very much, but at least we can hit them to send messages to each other instead of talking.

00:43:00.225 --> 00:43:04.428
They also make very good musical instruments.

00:43:04.488 --> 00:43:10.532
They can separate us from our families, but they can't take our roots from us.

00:43:10.592 --> 00:43:18.880
Africa is in our blood, and we'll always have our traditional dances to remember our culture and give us hope.

00:43:18.940 --> 00:43:25.145
Next week, some important visitors are coming to the mine, and we're going to entertain them.

00:43:25.205 --> 00:43:31.540
We'll sing songs about our lives, the bad working conditions, and our cruel boss.

00:43:31.600 --> 00:43:36.844
Of course, the visitors won't understand a word because they don't speak our language.

00:43:36.904 --> 00:43:48.108
CHAPTER 9 FOOD The influence of South Africa's different ethnic groups on local food makes a visit to a restaurant an interesting experience.

00:43:48.168 --> 00:43:53.189
Together, these cultural groups give the country its rainbow menu.

00:43:53.249 --> 00:44:02.651
The Bantu, as the blacks were known then, taught the early settlers how to grow vegetables and introduce them to modern agriculture.

00:44:02.711 --> 00:44:10.042
The Zulu people introduced the settlers to mealie meal, a soft cereal made from cornmeal.

00:44:10.102 --> 00:44:19.963
The slaves from the East, mostly from Malaysia, brought various spices with them and added interesting tastes to Dutch and English cooking.

00:44:20.023 --> 00:44:30.155
The French Huguenots came to the Cape and transformed part of the land into a fertile wine growing area called Franschhoek, French Corner.

00:44:30.215 --> 00:44:37.132
They planted vineyards there and turned South Africa into one of the world's leading producers of wine.

00:44:37.192 --> 00:44:42.942
The Germans introduced baked goods and cakes and the British brought meat pies.

00:44:43.002 --> 00:44:46.864
The Indians worked in the sugarcane fields in Durban.

00:44:46.924 --> 00:44:53.158
When their 10-year contracts were over, they stayed and Indian cooking became very popular.

00:44:53.218 --> 00:44:59.012
Today, bobotie is a national dish in South Africa.

00:44:59.072 --> 00:45:06.113
South Africans are meat lovers and no Sunday meal is complete without a braai.

00:45:06.173 --> 00:45:13.141
This is the South African word for a barbecue and almost every house has one in its garden.

00:45:13.201 --> 00:45:21.281
This tradition comes from the 17th century when the French Huguenots and Dutch settlers cook their meat on open fires.

00:45:21.341 --> 00:45:27.324
Today, a favourite braai meat is a German sausage made from B4 pork.

00:45:27.384 --> 00:45:33.058
South Africans call it boerewors, the African word for farmer's sausage.

00:45:33.118 --> 00:45:34.678
Bunny chow.

00:45:34.739 --> 00:45:40.969
In Durban, you can see street vendors selling some strange-looking bread wrapped in newspaper.

00:45:41.029 --> 00:45:48.118
Don't worry, bunny chow is a popular dish made with Indian curry, not baby rabbits.

00:45:48.178 --> 00:45:58.330
A hole is made in a quarter, half or whole loaf of white bread, and it is then filled with beef, chicken, lamb or bean curry.

00:45:58.390 --> 00:46:03.862
The sauce from the meat is absorbed by the bread, giving it a delicious taste.

00:46:03.922 --> 00:46:12.308
The origin of bunny chow isn't clear, but many believe Indian merchants first served it in a Durban restaurant.

00:46:12.368 --> 00:46:17.032
Under apartheid, certain cafes were only open to whites.

00:46:17.092 --> 00:46:22.304
Their solution was to serve Indian plantation workers through back windows.

00:46:22.364 --> 00:46:31.692
But the traditional Indian food was not easy to serve as fast food, so the cafe owners used bread filled with curry instead.

00:46:31.752 --> 00:46:37.545
A funny bunny is made from the middle of the loaf, so there's no hard bit at the bottom.

00:46:37.605 --> 00:46:43.186
You have to hold it firmly, or you'll soon find out why it's called a funny bunny.

00:46:43.246 --> 00:46:49.208
Biltong Biltong is dried meat, and South Africans love it.

00:46:49.268 --> 00:46:53.495
There are different types, like beef and ostrich biltong.

00:46:53.555 --> 00:46:55.841
Where does biltong come from?

00:46:55.901 --> 00:47:00.593
After a hunt, the San people took the meat and cut it into pieces.

00:47:00.653 --> 00:47:07.166
They hung it in the sun to dry for up to two weeks, so they would have meat when they really needed it.

00:47:07.226 --> 00:47:12.578
The Dutch also ate a kind of dried meat, but it was made a bit differently.

00:47:12.638 --> 00:47:15.810
They added spices and vinegar to preserve it.

00:47:15.870 --> 00:47:19.871
The Boers liked this idea and started to copy it.

00:47:19.932 --> 00:47:24.187
If you haven't tasted biltong, you haven't lived.

00:47:24.247 --> 00:47:31.672
mopane Worms Now you know about the Big Five, but have you heard of the Big Twelve?

00:47:31.732 --> 00:47:35.213
This refers to twelve African insects.

00:47:35.273 --> 00:47:41.535
The mopane Worm, the green and blue caterpillar of the Emperor Moth, is one of these.

00:47:41.595 --> 00:47:47.433
It feeds mainly on the leaves of the mopane tree, and that's the reason for its name.

00:47:47.493 --> 00:47:56.556
This fascinating little worm is a local delicacy for people in the northern parts of South Africa and is served in many restaurants.

00:47:56.616 --> 00:48:03.360
In local markets, you can see large bags of sun-dried mopane worms hanging in the shops.

00:48:03.420 --> 00:48:05.518
How are the worms cooked?

00:48:05.578 --> 00:48:12.302
Women and children pick the caterpillars by hand and then extract their green insides.

00:48:12.362 --> 00:48:16.624
Then they boil the worms and put them in the sun to dry.

00:48:16.684 --> 00:48:22.868
You can eat them fried or grilled or even cook them in a pot with onions and tomatoes.

00:48:22.928 --> 00:48:27.400
Some people like them dried and crunchy like potato crisps.

00:48:27.460 --> 00:48:38.608
Although not for the faint-hearted, a dish of mopane worms is a great substitute for a steak because they contain three times more protein than beef.

00:48:38.668 --> 00:48:45.440
15 worms a day give you all the calcium, iron and vitamin B2 you need.

00:48:45.500 --> 00:48:52.024
Just make sure they're the biggest fattest ones with the most protein.

00:48:52.084 --> 00:48:58.040
From the street markets to the townships and cities, art is all around.

00:48:58.100 --> 00:49:06.543
You can see it in the traditional African masks, the colourful beaded jewellery and the incredible township art.

00:49:06.603 --> 00:49:13.176
South African art comes in many different forms but every piece has one thing in common.

00:49:13.236 --> 00:49:15.396
It tells a story.

00:49:15.456 --> 00:49:23.832
Beading is a very popular art form in South Africa and you can see amazing beaded wire animals everywhere.

00:49:23.892 --> 00:49:29.898
These are mainly made by Zimbabwean immigrants and sold at the side of the road.

00:49:29.958 --> 00:49:38.342
Many of these immigrants were unable to find work and have used their creative skills to start their own roadside business.

00:49:38.402 --> 00:49:41.704
You can stop and buy one for a small price.

00:49:41.764 --> 00:49:51.264
It's not unusual to see tourists leaving South Africa carrying huge beaded giraffes or antelopes far too big to put in a suitcase.

00:49:51.324 --> 00:49:57.680
This woman is from the Ndebele people, descendants of Zulu and Xhosa peoples.

00:49:57.740 --> 00:50:02.082
The Ndebele are known for their traditional beadwork.

00:50:02.142 --> 00:50:08.156
Beaded clothes and jewelry are worn at ceremonies and are used to decorate things.

00:50:08.216 --> 00:50:12.388
As you can see, the woman is wearing a lot of beadwork.

00:50:12.448 --> 00:50:19.218
It's a sign of her status and you can be sure she sat for hours making all those clothes.

00:50:19.278 --> 00:50:33.478
She takes one bead at a time, carefully choosing the colours, and can use as many as 300,000 beads to produce a unique piece of art, telling the history of the Ndebele in pictures and symbols.

00:50:33.538 --> 00:50:42.590
When a Ndebele woman gets married, she often works for two to three years on a piece of beadwork for her future husband's parents.

00:50:42.650 --> 00:50:48.948
She knows the quality of her work is important if she wants to be accepted into the family.

00:50:49.008 --> 00:50:56.118
funeral clothes are also important, because they Ndebele believe in life after death.

00:50:56.178 --> 00:51:03.380
Over the years, they have incorporated materials and symbols from Western civilization into their art.

00:51:03.440 --> 00:51:08.176
Many people consider it the most impressive beadwork in the world.

00:51:08.236 --> 00:51:14.779
Masks are a very popular souvenir item in South Africa and sold in most fares.

00:51:14.839 --> 00:51:20.499
However, they are more representative of the rest of Africa than South Africa.

00:51:20.559 --> 00:51:30.096
Masks represent the spirit of an ancestor or god, and they were worn in ceremonies like crop harvesting, weddings and initiations.

00:51:30.156 --> 00:51:38.472
As early as the Stone Age, African masks were worn in religious ceremonies, celebrations and coming of age rights.

00:51:38.532 --> 00:51:42.436
These included praying, dancing and singing.

00:51:42.496 --> 00:51:50.892
Before a wooden mask was made, the mask maker purified himself and prayed for advice and help from his ancestors.

00:51:50.952 --> 00:51:54.724
He also offered a sacrifice to the tree's God.

00:51:54.784 --> 00:51:58.966
When the tree was cut down, it was left for a couple of days.

00:51:59.026 --> 00:52:02.868
This let the tree's spirits find a new resting place.

00:52:02.928 --> 00:52:07.787
Only after that, the masks were created from the wood of the tree.

00:52:07.847 --> 00:52:14.518
Today, African masks are made of exotic woods, animal skin or even metal.

00:52:14.578 --> 00:52:22.384
They are now considered to be valuable pieces of art and are desired by collectors for their historic and cultural value.

00:52:22.444 --> 00:52:27.947
You can see African masks in nearly all art museums throughout the world.

00:52:28.008 --> 00:52:32.732
Dumile Feni is one of South Africa's famous artists.

00:52:32.792 --> 00:52:39.088
He was born in the Western Cape in 1942 and became known for township art.

00:52:39.148 --> 00:52:47.766
This kind of art actually developed in the 1920s, but in those days it was called struggle or resistance art.

00:52:47.826 --> 00:52:53.458
At that time the laws of apartheid forced non-Europeans to live in townships.

00:52:53.518 --> 00:53:02.402
The conditions there were terrible and African artists started to show how they felt about apartheid with colourful paintings.

00:53:02.462 --> 00:53:10.826
There was a lot of poverty in the townships and oil paints were too expensive for many of the artists, so they improvised.

00:53:10.886 --> 00:53:14.968
This led to the birth of new township art genres.

00:53:15.028 --> 00:53:21.280
Dumile often drew on the back of old envelopes using nothing but a ballpoint pen.

00:53:21.340 --> 00:53:24.960
That's how he started his career as an artist.

00:53:25.020 --> 00:53:29.732
When apartheid ended, township art started to change.

00:53:29.792 --> 00:53:35.615
In the past, it reflected a country filled with desperate people and no hope.

00:53:35.675 --> 00:53:41.708
But artists in the New South Africa have developed their own unique form of township art.

00:53:41.768 --> 00:53:45.010
It's bright, colourful and diverse.

00:53:45.070 --> 00:53:48.862
They use all kinds of materials to create their art.

00:53:48.922 --> 00:53:58.286
Recycle plastic bags, old parts of bicycles, dried tea bags, soft drink cans and many other discarded treasures.

00:53:58.346 --> 00:54:06.660
CHAPTER 11 SPORT If you want to start a conversation with a South African, you can't go wrong with sport.

00:54:06.720 --> 00:54:12.283
South Africans love sport and once they start to talk, they can't stop.

00:54:12.343 --> 00:54:21.626
Although the country has produced some top-class athletes, South Africa hasn't always participated in international sporting events.

00:54:21.686 --> 00:54:29.078
During apartheid, South Africans weren't allowed to play sport internationally for 21 years.

00:54:29.138 --> 00:54:38.116
But once Nelson Mandela was set free, the sanctions were removed, and South Africa became a part of the world of global sport again.

00:54:38.176 --> 00:54:44.476
Since then, the country has hosted the football, cricket and rugby World Cups.

00:54:44.536 --> 00:54:50.160
Football To South Africans, football is more than just a game.

00:54:50.220 --> 00:54:52.222
It's almost a religion.

00:54:52.282 --> 00:54:56.855
It doesn't matter if you're black or white or what language you speak.

00:54:56.916 --> 00:55:03.022
Football, or soccer as they call it, is for everybody, even for prisoners.

00:55:03.082 --> 00:55:12.284
For a long time, prisoners weren't allowed to play football on Robben Island, so they made their own balls and played in their cells in secret.

00:55:12.344 --> 00:55:19.663
Finally, in 1965, the situation changed and prisoners were permitted to play football.

00:55:19.723 --> 00:55:28.457
The following year, five political prisoners formed the Makana Football Association and organized a league on the island.

00:55:28.517 --> 00:55:32.208
They proved that football wins over politics.

00:55:32.268 --> 00:55:42.512
Today, most of the players in South Africa's national team are black and the name of the team is Bafana Bafana, meaning the boys the boys.

00:55:42.572 --> 00:55:46.333
A local football game is a memorable experience.

00:55:46.393 --> 00:55:49.383
It sounds like there are elephants in the stadium.

00:55:49.443 --> 00:55:56.684
But it's just South African fans blowing on their vuvuzellas, long colourful plastic horns.

00:55:56.745 --> 00:56:02.216
Cricket Cricket is South Africa's second most popular sport.

00:56:02.276 --> 00:56:06.076
The Proteas is the name of the country's national team.

00:56:06.136 --> 00:56:09.580
The Protea is the national flower of South Africa.

00:56:09.640 --> 00:56:15.818
In the past, mostly English-speaking whites and Indian South Africans play cricket.

00:56:15.879 --> 00:56:18.310
But this is slowly changing.

00:56:18.370 --> 00:56:23.914
More and more black people are starting to take up cricket at a young age.

00:56:23.975 --> 00:56:29.079
The World Cup is the most important international cricket competition.

00:56:29.139 --> 00:56:34.761
It takes place every four years and the best cricket players take part.

00:56:34.822 --> 00:56:38.742
The first World Cup was in 1975.

00:56:38.802 --> 00:56:44.746
In this competition, the West Indies weren't against Australia and took home the trophy.

00:56:44.806 --> 00:56:50.620
South Africa weren't allowed to participate in the World Cup until 1992.

00:56:50.680 --> 00:56:57.614
But when they did, they showed the world their talent, winning against Australia in the opening match.

00:56:57.674 --> 00:57:02.527
When it comes to international cricket, it's hard to beat South Africa.

00:57:02.588 --> 00:57:08.414
They have had the title of best or second best team in the world for many years.

00:57:08.474 --> 00:57:17.169
Rugby The Springboks, South Africa's national rugby team take their name from the country's national animal.

00:57:17.229 --> 00:57:18.917
A kind of deer.

00:57:18.977 --> 00:57:24.422
In the past, Rugby was considered a white man's sport, played by Afrikaners.

00:57:24.482 --> 00:57:26.733
But today it's a game for all.

00:57:26.793 --> 00:57:31.196
And once again, South Africa competes internationally.

00:57:31.256 --> 00:57:34.419
Hi, I'm François Pienaar.

00:57:34.479 --> 00:57:39.132
I was captain of the Springboks from 1993 to 1996.

00:57:39.192 --> 00:57:44.161
And I led them to victory when we hosted the Rugby World Cup in 1995.

00:57:44.222 --> 00:57:55.298
Before that, Australia never lost a single game, but we beat them and went on to win against the All Blacks, New Zealand, in the final.

00:57:55.358 --> 00:58:02.004
We took the trophy by three points, and it was one of the most memorable moments in history.

00:58:02.064 --> 00:58:07.659
After the match, there was a ceremony, and of course, Nelson Mandela attended.

00:58:07.720 --> 00:58:12.404
He was wearing my number six shirt and he embraced me.

00:58:12.464 --> 00:58:17.270
In that one moment, 400 years of suffering disappeared.

00:58:17.330 --> 00:58:20.362
It shows you the power of sport.

00:58:20.422 --> 00:58:24.524
I felt so proud to be South African.

00:58:24.584 --> 00:58:31.867
The Rugby Championship, once known as the Tri-Nations, is another important competition.

00:58:31.927 --> 00:58:37.471
The winning team is known as the best international team in the Southern Hemisphere.

00:58:37.532 --> 00:58:45.548
From 1996 to 2011, South Africa, Australia and New Zealand competed in this event.

00:58:45.608 --> 00:58:54.058
These three countries have won six out of the seven World Cups, so it is considered an honour to win the Tri-Nations.

00:58:54.118 --> 00:59:02.950
In 2012, Argentina were also included in this championship.

00:59:03.010 --> 00:59:16.482
The current flag of the Republic of South Africa was adopted on 27 April 1994 after Nelson Mandela was elected president.

00:59:16.542 --> 00:59:18.923
It replaced the old flag from 1928.

00:59:18.983 --> 00:59:27.376
The new national flag, designed by Frederick Brownell, was chosen to represent the new democracy.

00:59:27.436 --> 00:59:31.504
The only symbolism in the flag is the Y shape.

00:59:31.564 --> 00:59:40.108
Some say this represents the uniting of different elements within South African society and moving forward in unity.

00:59:40.168 --> 00:59:47.054
Three of the colours, black, green and yellow, are in the flag of the African National Congress.

00:59:47.114 --> 00:59:56.831
The other three, red, white and blue, are used in the old flag of the Transvaal, the flag of the Netherlands and the flag of the United Kingdom.

00:59:56.891 --> 01:00:01.696
White and blue were also found in the old flag of South Africa.

01:00:01.756 --> 01:00:03.510
Crime.

01:00:03.570 --> 01:00:07.244
Crime is an enormous problem in South Africa today.

01:00:07.304 --> 01:00:11.097
The black and white middle-class communities live in fear.

01:00:11.157 --> 01:00:19.569
Their houses are protected by high walls with electric fencing, guard dogs, bars on the window and alarm systems.

01:00:19.629 --> 01:00:22.404
Unemployment levels are very high.

01:00:22.464 --> 01:00:33.190
Because of this, criminal behaviour has become normal for many young South Africans and approximately 13,000 children are arrested every month for crime.

01:00:33.250 --> 01:00:38.663
There are around 43 murders a day per 100,000 people.

01:00:38.723 --> 01:00:43.776
Most victims are the owners of expensive cars and elegant houses.

01:00:43.836 --> 01:00:52.200
In eight out of ten residential robberies, domestic workers, gardeners and former employees are involved in the planning.

01:00:52.261 --> 01:00:56.923
Hunger and poverty is the motive of 35% of robbers.

01:00:56.983 --> 01:01:03.786
Most robbers rob 103 times over a period of 7 years before they are caught.

01:01:03.846 --> 01:01:14.228
Poverty As you leave Johannesburg or Cape Town, you can't ignore the slums at the borders of the cities and in rural areas.

01:01:14.288 --> 01:01:20.306
Here you can find around half a million people living in very poor conditions.

01:01:20.366 --> 01:01:23.940
Many still live without electricity and water.

01:01:24.000 --> 01:01:28.632
A short drive around the suburbs of Johannesburg will reveal the truth.

01:01:28.692 --> 01:01:32.806
There is still a very clear division between rich and poor.

01:01:32.866 --> 01:01:39.969
This is still causing problems in South Africa, almost 20 years after the end of apartheid.

01:01:40.029 --> 01:01:41.718
AIDS.

01:01:41.779 --> 01:01:45.250
AIDS is one of South Africa's greatest problems.

01:01:45.310 --> 01:01:51.244
More than 5.6 million people in South Africa today are HIV positive.

01:01:51.304 --> 01:01:54.896
That's about 10% of the total population.

01:01:54.956 --> 01:02:00.208
It has the highest number of HIV AIDS cases in the world.

01:02:00.268 --> 01:02:11.134
South Africa is trying to reduce the number of new HIV infections and has expanded treatment for people living with HIV, especially pregnant women.

01:02:11.194 --> 01:02:18.290
Last year, more than 260,000 people with AIDS died.

01:02:18.350 --> 01:02:27.897
Mandela's dream was to create a country free of poverty and social inequality.

01:02:27.957 --> 01:02:31.138
But his dream is far from the reality.

01:02:31.198 --> 01:02:36.180
Modern South Africa has a long way to go to achieve Mandela's dream.

01:02:36.240 --> 01:02:43.806
Although a truly democratic South Africa seems far away, with careful planning, it can happen.

01:02:43.866 --> 01:02:50.584
The first step is to reduce unemployment and to increase the standard of education for black pupils.

01:02:50.644 --> 01:03:02.790
An extra 12 million pupils in public schools now have the right to basic education and in higher education financial help for studies is available for poor students.

01:03:02.850 --> 01:03:11.614
Better social conditions and health education will help reduce the number of AIDS victims and create a healthier population.

01:03:11.674 --> 01:03:24.245
Since April 2010, 500,000 new patients have been given treatment and HIV AIDS deaths have been reduced by 50% to fewer than 270,000.

01:03:24.305 --> 01:03:33.595
The transmission of HIV from mother to child in South Africa has begun to decrease in recent years from 8% in 2010 to 2.7% in 2013.

01:03:33.635 --> 01:03:40.310
Finally, with the help of foreign investors, the economy will grow.

01:03:40.370 --> 01:04:01.564
If these steps are taken, maybe 20 years from now, the dream of a democratic South Africa will replace today's reality and Nelson Mandela's hopes and dreams will come true.

01:04:01.624 --> 01:04:19.146
The 2009 film Invictus, based on a true story, is a drama about the events surrounding the 1995 Rugby World Cup in South Africa.

01:04:19.206 --> 01:04:35.176
Directed by Clint Eastwood, this inspiring film starring Morgan Freeman as Nelson Mandela and Matt Damon as Springboks rugby captain François Pienaar showed how sport could unite different people.

01:04:35.236 --> 01:04:38.738
The film is set in 1995.

01:04:38.799 --> 01:04:50.020
Mandela was president of South Africa and apartheid was at an end, but the country was in turmoil and there was a lot of hatred between the blacks and the whites.

01:04:50.080 --> 01:04:54.271
South Africa was going to host the Rugby World Cup.

01:04:54.331 --> 01:05:00.202
The blacks hated the South African rugby team because it was dominated by whites.

01:05:00.262 --> 01:05:05.290
But Mandela was determined to change this with the help of François Pienaar.

01:05:05.350 --> 01:05:17.112
He persuaded the mainly Black South African Sports Committee to support the Springboks and met with François Pienaar, inspiring the captain to conquer hate with rugby.

01:05:17.172 --> 01:05:27.528
The highly motivated François Pienaar worked hard preparing his team for the World Cup and slowly won the support of black people around the country.

01:05:27.588 --> 01:05:34.153
By the time the big day arrived, François Pienaar proved that Rugby could unite a divided nation.

01:05:34.213 --> 01:05:41.960
And in the final, the Springboks led a more united South Africa to victory over New Zealand.

01:05:42.020 --> 01:05:46.834
Page 66, cross-curricular focus.

01:05:46.894 --> 01:05:56.260
Skin Skin, 2008, was the tragic true story of Sandra Laing.

01:05:56.321 --> 01:06:06.470
Although her parents were white Afrikaners, Sandra's skin was dark and her hair was curly, an unusual situation.

01:06:06.531 --> 01:06:11.514
But in a country with apartheid, this was a disaster.

01:06:11.574 --> 01:06:16.974
The young Sandra became an outcast because she had dark skin.

01:06:17.034 --> 01:06:25.440
In 1965, at the age of 10, she was classified as coloured and removed from school.

01:06:25.500 --> 01:06:33.922
Sandra's father, Abraham, couldn't accept this, and he fought for her to be reclassified as white.

01:06:33.982 --> 01:06:45.856
He succeeded, but at that time in South Africa, the colour of your skin dictated your life, and Sandra was never truly accepted by the white community.

01:06:45.917 --> 01:06:56.936
Sandra felt more comfortable in the company of blacks, and Abraham was dismayed when she fell in love with a black man named Petrus and married him.

01:06:56.996 --> 01:07:03.726
Abraham disowned Sandra and didn't allow his wife's Sannie to communicate with their daughter.

01:07:03.786 --> 01:07:09.542
But Sandra's marriage didn't last because Petrus became physically abusive.

01:07:09.602 --> 01:07:13.426
Sandra took their two children and left.

01:07:13.486 --> 01:07:19.748
When apartheid came to an end, there was renewed interest in Sandra's story.

01:07:19.808 --> 01:07:24.588
She appeared in an interview and Sannie saw her on TV.

01:07:24.649 --> 01:07:28.884
She wrote to Sandra, telling her of her father's death.

01:07:28.944 --> 01:07:37.013
The two were finally reunited after 29 years apart, only a short time before Sannie's death.